One more check: in the module 4.3.4 practice, `cx.on_app_quit(|cx| async move { ... cx.background_executor()... })` — the closure param `cx: &mut App`, captured by the async block via move? `cx` is `&mut App` — you can't move a `&mut` into an async block and use it after the closure returns... Actually `on_app_quit` signature: `on_quit: impl FnMut(&mut App) -> Fut`. The closure receives `&mut App` and returns a future. The future must be 'static (`Fut: 'static + Future<Output = ()>`). So `async move { cx.background_executor()... }` — cx is `&mut App` which is NOT 'static; moving a borrow into the future won't compile!

Wait — but the future needs to be 'static. So the pattern must be: capture clones of what you need before creating the future. E.g.:

```rust
cx.on_app_quit(|cx| {
    let executor = cx.background_executor().clone();
    async move {
        eprintln!("...");
        executor.timer(Duration::from_millis(500)).await;
        ...
    }
})
